      "textEn": "Like safe; not—no; Esham—their; Vayam—we; Na cha—not; Vayah—infinite time; Prabhavam—are capable;\nDande—in giving punishment. My dear flock! Do not go to such devotees, because they are completely surrendered to the lotus feet of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. They are impartial and their stories\nare sung by the gods and the inhabitants of Siddhaloka. You people also don't go near them.\nGo. They are always protected by the mace of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, therefore Brahma and I\nand even time are not capable of punishing them. Meaning: In other words, Yama, the god of death, said to his servants, \"My dear servants! Before this,\nyou may have been doing whatever you wanted to harass the devotees, but now stop it. Those\ndevotees who have taken refuge in the lotus feet of God and who constantly chant the name of God,\ntheir praise is done by the gods and even the inhabitants of Siddha Loka.\nThose devotees are so venerable and high\nthat Lord Vishnu himself protects them, holding a mace in his hand. Therefore, this time, whatever you have\ndone, neglecting it, from now on, never go to such a devotee, otherwise you\nwill be killed by the mace of Lord Vishnu. Therefore, this is my warning. Lord Vishnu\nGives punishment to his devotees with mace and chakra. You people, by molesting the devotees, do not take the risk of punishment.\nDo not take the risk. If you, if Brahma or even me have to punish them, then Lord Vishnu\nwill punish us. So don't bother your devotees anymore.Tanayadhvamsato vimukhanmukunda-\nPadaravindamakarandarasadajastram.\nWithout desires, in the company of the supremely accomplished,\nIn the house of the unyielding, in the posture of liberation, bound by thirst. 28 ”. Meaning of the word:\n\nTaan—to them; Aanayadhvam—bring before me; Asat—not devotee (who has not accepted Krishna Bhava Namrut);\nVimukhan—those who are averse; Mukunda—the complete Supreme Personality of Godhead, Mukunda; Pada-arvinda—of the lotus feet; Makaranda—from honey; Rasat—from taste; Ajastram—continuously; Nishkinchaina-him—by men completely free from material attachment; Paramahamsa-kula-him—by the family of Paramahamsa"
