      "textEn": "They remain engaged in Vedic rituals, sometimes performing penance rites. Even then,\ndue to being incompletely purified, they repeatedly return to sinful deeds. Meaning: It is the duty of a devotee to utter the Hare Krishna Mantra. He can perform Kirtan sometimes with crime and\nsometimes without crime, but if he adopts this method with seriousness,\nthen he will attain that accomplishment which cannot be attained through the Vedic rituals of atonement. Those who are attached to the Vedic rituals, but do not believe in devotion, those who\nadvise atonement, but do not praise the chanting of the Lord's name, they remain deprived of attaining the supreme perfection. Therefore, devotees, while completely detached from material pleasures, never\nrenounce Krishna consciousness and nectar in favor of Vedic rituals. Those who are attached to the Vedic rituals\nbecause of lust, they have to repeatedly endure the sufferings of the material world.\nare.\nKing Parikshit compared their actions to the act of a mahout washing his hands, that is, an elephant's bath. Thus, remembering the glory of the Lord,\nWith awe-struck minds, you are the Yama-kinkara. The one who is the refuge of the new, the one who is the enemy,\nAnd the one who is the seer, and the one who is the enemy, and the one who is the king after that. 34). Meaning of the word\n\nIttham—Of such power; Swa-bhartru-gaditam—As told by one's own lord (Yama Raja); Bhagavat-Mahitvam—Complete\nPurushottam, God, and their names, fame, The unique glory of form and qualities; remembered—by recalling; Vismit-dhiyah—with wonder;\nThose whose minds are filled with wonder; Yam-kinkarah—all the servants of Yamaraja; Te—they; Na—not; Eva—indeed; Achyut-ashraya—\nJanam—Indeed, that person who has taken refuge at the lotus feet of Krishna; Prati-shankamanaah—Always fearful; Drashtum—To see;\nCha—and; Bibhyanti—Are fearful; Tatah-prathimithi—Starting from then; Sma—Indeed; Rajan—Oh King..\n\nHearing about the unique g